openstack-cinder — OpenStack Block Storage (Cinder) 29.0.0 (2026.2 Hibiscus)
============================================================================

This package installs the Cinder block storage service: API, scheduler,
volume and backup managers. It installs software ONLY: nothing is
enabled, started, migrated or credentialed at install time.

What it ships
-------------

  /usr/bin/cinder-*                administrative CLIs and daemons
                                   (api, backup, manage, scheduler,
                                   status, volume, rtstool, ...)
  cinder.wsgi.api:application      the supported WSGI application
  cinder/db/migrations/versions/   database migration scripts
  /etc/cinder/api-paste.ini        upstream paste pipeline (wiring)
  /etc/cinder/rootwrap.conf + rootwrap.d/volume.filters
  /etc/cinder/resource_filters.json scheduler hints (read-only data)
  /etc/init.d/cinder-*             OpenRC services (4 daemons)
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/cinder-*.service   systemd services (4 daemons)
  /etc/conf.d/cinder-api           WSGI container runtime settings (api)

Operator steps (NOT performed by this package)
----------------------------------------------

1. Write /etc/cinder/cinder.conf from the samples and the generator
   inputs in /usr/share/cinder/:

     oslo-config-generator \
       --config-file /usr/share/cinder/cinder-config-generator.conf \
       --output-file /etc/cinder/cinder.conf
     # edit /etc/cinder/cinder.conf: database, keystone auth,
     # enabled_backends + one section per backend (LVM, Ceph, NFS,
     # ZFS, ...) and a backup section. No database driver is installed
     # by this package: add python-pymysql for MariaDB, or use sqlite
     # for trials.

2. Provision the backing store OUTSIDE the installer. ZFS example
   (saphira-zfs-userspace is a package dependency, the pool is the
   operator's explicit decision):

     zpool create cinder-pool mirror /dev/sdX /dev/sdY
     # then reference cinder-pool in the [zfs] backend section.

   iSCSI export uses the kernel LIO target through rtslib-fb over
   configfs (targetcli is the optional interactive frontend).

3. Initialise the database:

     cinder-manage db sync

4. Register the service and endpoints in Keystone (operator-chosen).

5. Enable per node role, explicitly. Controller example:

     rc-update add cinder-api default && rc-service cinder-api start
     rc-update add cinder-scheduler default && ...
   Storage node example:

     rc-update add cinder-volume default && rc-service cinder-volume start
     rc-update add cinder-backup default && ...
   (systemd: systemctl enable --now cinder-<daemon> instead.)

Independence
------------

Cinder depends on the native python closure, gunicorn, ZFS userspace
and database client libraries. It does not depend on Nova, Neutron,
Glance or Horizon, and it can be installed on its own.
